readings.c2s3:
''mate''; mod. E. directly < L. ''aroma'', < Gr. ἄρωμα (ἀρωματ-), any spice or sweet herb; perhaps orig. the smell of a plowed field, and so identical with ἄρωμα, a plowed field, arable land, < ἀρόειν, plow, = L. ''arare'', plow: see ''arable''.] '''1†.''' Spice: usually in the plural, spices. ''N. E. D.''—'''2.''' An odor arising from spices, plants, or other substances, more especially an agreeable odor; fragrance; spicy perfume.
{{smaller block|The air had the true northern ''aroma''.
{{right|''B. Taylor'', Lands of the Saracen, p. 276.}}}}
'''3.''' Figuratively, a characteristic but subtle quality; a pervasive charm or flavor.
{{smaller block|The subtle ''aroma'' of genius. {{right|''Saturday Rev.''}}
A happy surprise awaits those who come to the study of the early literature of New England with the expectation of finding it altogether arid in sentiment, or void of the spirit and ''aroma'' of poetry.
{{right|''M. C. Tyler'', Hist. Amer. Lit., I. 264.}}}}
='''Syn. 2.''' ''Perfume'', ''Fragrance'', etc. See ''smell'', ''n.''

'''aromatic''' (ar-ọ̄-mat′ik), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''aromatick'', ''-yque'', < ME. ''aromatyk'', < OF. ''aromatique'', < LL. ''aromaticus'', < Gr. ἀρωματικός, < ἄρωμα, spice, sweet herb: see ''aroma''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Giving out an aroma; fragrant; sweet-scented; odoriferous; of spicy flavor.
{{smaller block|Great blueberry bushes hanging thick with misty blue spheres, ''aromatic'' and sweet with a sweetness no tropic suns can give. ''R. T. Cooke'', Somebody’s Neighbors, p. 291.}}
'''2.''' Caused by an aroma or fragrant odor.
{{smaller block|Die of a rose in ''aromatic'' pain.
{{right|''Pope'', Essay on Man, l. 200.}}}}
